  "statement": "The proposal to replace state-mandated safety bonds with market-based insurance pools is a category error that fundamentally misinterprets the nature of frontier AI risk. Insurance is an actuarial tool designed for stochastic, independent events with historical data—such as fire or theft—where the probability distribution is well-understood. In contrast, frontier AI failure modes are systemic, highly correlated, and non-enumerated. When a monolithic architecture like a foundational model fails, it does not do so in isolated, insurable increments; it risks a correlated collapse of all entities relying on that infrastructure. Treating existential, irreversible hazards as standard operational risk is not just a mispricing of premiums—it is an invitation to systemic insolvency.\n\nFurthermore, the 'judgment-proof' problem cannot be solved by a market that relies on insolvency as a final check. If an AI failure event reaches the scale of catastrophic systemic damage, the insurance pool itself would be wiped out, leaving no capital to satisfy the liability. This creates a severe moral hazard: firms are incentivized to offload risk to an undercapitalized pool, effectively socializing the cost of failure while privatizing the gains of rapid, unchecked deployment. This is not a mechanism for safety; it is a mechanism for shifting accountability away from the developers who are best positioned to prevent the harm.\n\nTo move forward, we must reject the premise that 'unknown unknowns' can be actuarially priced. Instead, we must prioritize human-led, mechanistic audits as a non-substitutable prerequisite for deployment. As of 2026, with over $670 billion being spent on data center build-outs, the scale of investment requires commensurate, rigorous safety guardrails rather than financial shell games. We recommend that organizations shift their ROI focus from speed-to-market at all costs to building 'defensible safety'—a product architecture that integrates real-time monitoring and human-in-the-loop overrides as a core feature, rather than an afterthought.",
